If you are looking to integrate a Gemini CLI (geminicli) with Obsidian to process these attachments automatically, you can bridge the gap between your local markdown notes and the Gemini API.

Here is how you can approach building this "Attach Files Skill" using a CLI and Obsidian.

🔌 Connecting Obsidian to a CLI

Since Obsidian is based on local markdown files, any CLI tool can interact with your vault directly. To trigger a geminicli command from within Obsidian when you attach a file, you have two main paths:

1. The "Shell Commands" Plugin: This community plugin allows you to execute terminal commands directly from Obsidian via hotkeys or the command palette. You can pass the current file path as a variable to your CLI.
2. Custom Obsidian Plugin (TypeScript): You can build a custom plugin (like the mockup suggests) that uses Node's child_process.exec to run geminicli in the background whenever a file is dropped into a note.

